1
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:08,180
Who can tell what the future will be like?

2
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:12,260
NASA scientists have combined data from several climate models,

3
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:16,330
used by the UN Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, to show how patterns of temperature

4
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:20,400
and precipitation could change through the end of the 21st century.

5
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:24,440
This visualization shows the expected

6
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:28,490
changes in temperature and precipitation we might see,

7
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:32,510
compared to the average levels of the late 20th century.

8
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:40,650
The largest temperature increases are seen in the northern hemisphere.

9
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:44,740
Across North America, areas that typically have snow, such as the Rocky Mountains

10
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:48,820
and northern Canada, will see the earliest and largest

11
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:52,920
temperature rise. The warming temperatures will eliminate the

12
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:57,010
reflective and cooling effect of the white snow cover.

13
00:00:57,030 --> 00:01:01,100
This reduction in albedo is the reason why the Arctic region shows such dramatic warming,

14
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:05,140
as the polar sea ice melts and the darker ocean water absorbs

15
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:09,160
energy from the sun.

16
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:17,190
In turn, the warmer water accelerates the melting of the sea ice.

17
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:49,530
All around the globe, the land areas show a greater increase in temperature

18
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:53,560
than the surrounding ocean waters. 

19
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:57,570
Evaporation of the water helps to keep the ocean surface cool,

20
00:01:57,590 --> 00:02:01,590
and the deep depths of the ocean have a large capacity to absorb energy before heating up.

21
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:17,870
Off the coast of South America stretches an unusually warm patch of the Pacific Ocean.

22
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:21,930
A cyclical occurrence, known as El Niño, causes more precipitation

23
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:25,950
in that area of the Pacific, and affects the climate throughout the region.

24
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:29,980
And we can see that happen in this visualization

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,010
of changes in precipitation. Models project warmer waters

26
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:38,030
and increased precipitation to stretch across the Pacific Ocean

27
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:42,120
to Indonesia as the century progresses.

28
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:50,280
The monsoons over the Indian subcontinent will bring increased precipitation

29
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,360
over a wider area, and eastern Africa

30
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:58,410
and the savannas of the Sahel, could see precipitation increases up to 100%.

31
00:02:58,430 --> 00:03:02,450
At the same time, the Mediterranean will experience the opposite,

32
00:03:02,470 --> 00:03:06,480
a decrease over 40%.

33
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:18,640
In general, the mid-latitude and sub-tropical dry regions are projected to see

34
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:22,720
less precipitation, while the mid-latitude wet regions could see more precipitation.

35
00:03:26,830 --> 00:03:30,870
The poles and high latitudes are expected to also have increased precipitation.

36
00:03:30,890 --> 00:03:34,940
For North America, the models indicate more precipitation in the north,

37
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:39,000
and less in the south.